ltIsValueLt : (x y : ℕᵇ) → x < y → toℕ x ℕ.< toℕ y
-- Base case: y = ε. By the clauses, no x satisfies x < ε, so the statement holds vacuously.
ltIsValueLt x zero p = {!!}
-- Inductive case y = b0 y′ (b0 = 2[1+_]), with x = ε. Then ε < b0 y′ gives ε < y′, and the induction hypothesis gives 0 = val ε < val y′. Multiplying by 2 gives 0 < val (b0 y′).
ltIsValueLt zero (2[1+ y' ]) p = {!!}
-- Inductive case y = b0 y′, with x = b0 x′. Then b0 x′ < b0 y′ gives x′ < y′, so by the induction hypothesis val x′ < val y′. Multiplying by 2 gives val (b0 x′) < val (b0 y′).
ltIsValueLt (2[1+ x' ]) (2[1+ y' ]) p = {!!}
-- Inductive case y = b0 y′, with x = b1 x′ (b1 = 1+[2_]). Then b1 x′ < b0 y′ gives x′ < y′, so the induction hypothesis gives a := val x′ < b := val y′. Since a + 1 ≤ b, we get 2a + 1 < 2a + 2 ≤ 2b, that is val (b1 x′) < val (b0 y′).
ltIsValueLt (1+[2 x' ]) (2[1+ y' ]) p = {!!}
-- Inductive case y = b1 y′, with x = ε. Since ε < b1 y′ always holds, we only need 0 < 2 · val y′ + 1, which holds because 2 · val y′ + 1 ≥ 1 > 0.
ltIsValueLt zero (1+[2 y' ]) p = {!!}
-- Inductive case y = b1 y′, with x = b0 x′. Then b0 x′ < b1 y′ gives x′ ≤ y′. If x′ = y′, then 2a < 2a + 1 = 2b + 1. If x′ < y′, the induction hypothesis gives a < b, so 2a < 2b ≤ 2b + 1. Hence val (b0 x′) < val (b1 y′).
ltIsValueLt (2[1+ x' ]) (1+[2 y' ]) p = {!!}
-- Inductive case y = b1 y′, with x = b1 x′. Then b1 x′ < b1 y′ gives x′ < y′, so the induction hypothesis gives a := val x′ < b := val y′. Hence 2a + 1 < 2b + 1, that is val (b1 x′) < val (b1 y′).
ltIsValueLt (1+[2 x' ]) (1+[2 y' ]) p = {!!}

ltIsValueLt-gap6 : (x' : ℕᵇ) → (y' : ℕᵇ) → (p : 1+[2 x' ] < 1+[2 y' ]) → toℕ 1+[2 x' ] ℕ.< toℕ 1+[2 y' ]
